<section class="engagements chapter shell" id="ways-to-work" aria-labelledby="engagement-title">
  <div class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Three ways to engage</p><h2 id="engagement-title">The help depends<br />on the problem.</h2></div>
  <div class="engagement-list">
    <article><h3>Advisory<span aria-hidden="true">01</span></h3><div><p>Think through the problem with you. Frame the decision, test the assumptions, weigh the options, and choose a direction.</p><p class="engagement-outcome">Sometimes the right decision is the whole engagement.</p></div></article>
    <article><h3>Design<span aria-hidden="true">02</span></h3><div><p>Figure out what should change: who owns what, how decisions get made, and how people, systems, and customer experience fit together.</p><p class="engagement-outcome">A design the people doing the work can actually use.</p></div></article>
    <article><h3>Build<span aria-hidden="true">03</span></h3><div><p>Make the change real. That can mean systems, software, integrations, automation, documentation, or hands-on execution.</p><p class="engagement-outcome">Implementation when it serves the answer.</p></div></article>
  </div>
  <p class="engagement-note">You work directly with me. We agree on the scope around what needs resolving. An advisory engagement does not have to become a build.</p>
</section>
